00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.530
which will to be opening in early 2024

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:57.780
with galleries completely redone,

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:01.023
about 50% larger than before
and it will be completely free.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.100
We've got new mounts of
just about everything,

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.440
a new K to 12 education center.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.883
Can't wait to welcome you there.

00:02:09.883 --> 00:02:14.883
So fossils are part of the
stony fabric of our planet.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.417
They're component of what
we call the rock record,

00:02:18.417 --> 00:02:20.331
and in that way they're geological,

00:02:20.331 --> 00:02:22.380
both in the compositional sense,

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.598
in the sense that they're
studied by geologists,

00:02:24.598 --> 00:02:28.110
but they're also part of
the book of life's history

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.030
and are in that way biologic

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.700
and can be viewed through
a biological lens.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.270
To be a paleontologist is to
try to straddle both worlds,

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.969
often badly, always uncomfortably,

00:02:38.969 --> 00:02:42.420
but there's some who truly master this.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.420
OC Marsh and John Ostrom
both were able to muster

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.941
their knowledge of the living world

00:02:47.941 --> 00:02:52.941
to gain insight to illuminate
the lives of extinct animals.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.560
In fact, I think Ostrom's finest work

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.930
was his work on the evolution of flight,

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:59.610
which was so informed by the behavior

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.010
of living birds, living bats.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.810
Ostrom had the great insight that birds

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:05.490
are descended from dinosaurs.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.830
And a successor here at Yale, Godiet,

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:08.880
who's in the audience today,

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.845
showed rigorously that birds are dinosaurs

00:03:10.845 --> 00:03:13.350
and that the characteristics
of extinct creatures

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:15.570
can be inferred from a particular kind

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.988
of systematic examination
of their living relatives.

00:03:18.988 --> 00:03:22.693
But no one in the modern
age, in my opinion,

00:03:22.693 --> 00:03:25.650
has done more for the
understanding of dinosaurs

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.851
and other extinct
vertebrates as living animals

00:03:27.851 --> 00:03:32.851
than Professor Lawrence 'Larry'
Witmer of Ohio University.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.660
Larry is an anatomist par excellence,

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.569
and that is perhaps to
me the highest compliment

00:03:38.569 --> 00:03:41.627
one can pay a biologist,
for anatomy is 3D,

00:03:41.627 --> 00:03:44.640
nay 4D structure and is the basis

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.010
of everything living things can do.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.534
Consider that even the
twistings and foldings of DNA

00:03:49.534 --> 00:03:51.930
are anatomy writ small.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.270
The hardest problems in the
life sciences and medicine

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:58.230
have to do with biotic
geometry, with space and time.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.303
Larry has his bachelor's from Cornell,

00:04:00.303 --> 00:04:03.630
Masters from KU, PhD from Johns Hopkins.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:05.670
He holds the Chang
Ying-Chien Professorship

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:07.440
of Paleontology at Ohio

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.486
and he's published
numerous groundbreaking,

00:04:09.486 --> 00:04:13.380
I would say, discipline-creating papers

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:15.930
in all the most exclusive
scientific journalists.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:19.500
He's appeared National
Geographic, CNN, NPR,

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.780
media around the world.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.190
He is highly dedicated
also to public education

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.690
at all levels.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:31.290
His scholarly work has
been a primary inspiration,

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:32.702
maybe the primary inspiration

00:04:32.702 --> 00:04:36.075
for my own lab's work here at Yale.

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:39.583
And so it's with very great
pleasure that I, that we,

00:04:39.583 --> 00:04:42.690
give him to you for Professor Witmer

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:45.660
is a living time machine
who will bring you

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:48.059
as close as one can come to dinosaurs,

00:04:48.059 --> 00:04:51.753
not buried in the earth,
but alive here before you

00:04:51.753 --> 00:04:56.753
once more Larry, my friend.
(audience claps)

00:05:01.696 --> 00:05:03.960
- Well, thanks Anjan.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.950
I hope I can live up to that introduction.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:09.417
It is a tremendous honor for me

00:05:09.417 --> 00:05:14.190
to be invited to give the
inaugural John H Ostrom lecture

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:18.505
here in the the glorious
OC Marsh lecture hall

00:05:18.505 --> 00:05:22.290
right next to the legendary
Yale Peabody Museum.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:25.110
We all can't wait for that to open,

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:26.220
and you should all be thrilled

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.230
that it's actually
gonna be free admission.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:29.480
That's a wonderful thing.

00:05:31.028 --> 00:05:36.028
So the wonderful thing about John Ostrom

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:40.120
is that I actually knew John Ostrom.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.380
I got to know him during my visits here

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:46.933
to the Peabody Museum, I'd
see him at conferences,

00:05:46.933 --> 00:05:51.180
I took this picture here back in 1985

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:55.662
when I was a Master student
at the University of Kansas.

00:05:55.662 --> 00:05:58.980
John Ostrom has always
been sort of a hero of mine

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.680
when I was growing up, so
it kind of blew my mind

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:02.869
when I would go to a conference

00:06:02.869 --> 00:06:05.970
and he'd walk up to me
and go, "hi, Larry!"

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:09.690
I'd go like, "John Ostrom knows my name?

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:11.422
And he wants me to call him John?"

00:06:11.422 --> 00:06:13.830
Just couldn't quite handle that.

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:15.437
And it turns out that we're related,

00:06:15.437 --> 00:06:20.437
at least academically.
(audience laughs)

00:06:20.997 --> 00:06:25.997
John Ostrom did his PhD
work under the mentorship

00:06:27.172 --> 00:06:31.050
of Ned Colbert at the American
Museum of Natural History.

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:34.503
Later John was the PhD
advisor for Peter Dodson

00:06:34.503 --> 00:06:39.503
and who was in turn the PhD
advisor for Dave Weishampel,

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:45.060
who was my PhD advisor at
Johns Hopkins Medical School.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:49.900
So that would make John my
academic, great grandparent

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:53.310
And that's an amazing picture,

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.473
except I really wish I were in it!

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.433
There, I fixed it.
(audience laughs)

00:06:59.433 --> 00:07:01.342
You do what you can.

00:07:01.342 --> 00:07:06.342
So I'm not gonna fully recount
John's illustrious career,

00:07:07.029 --> 00:07:11.806
but I do wanna point out out
why his work is so revered.

00:07:11.806 --> 00:07:14.876
Many, many achievements,
certainly many contributions,

00:07:14.876 --> 00:07:19.283
but he was a key player
in two major debates,

00:07:19.283 --> 00:07:22.500
both of which have been so
important and transformative

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:23.640
for our discipline.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.597
One was the origin of birds

00:07:25.597 --> 00:07:30.597
needed absolutely seminal
work on Archaeopteryx.

00:07:30.957 --> 00:07:35.957
And he was a major player in
the sort of the revolution

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:39.930
in dinosaur physiology and behavior,

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:42.959
drawing largely on his
work on Deinonychus,

00:07:42.959 --> 00:07:45.810
a dinosaur he discovered and named

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:47.280
and which really is the model

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:50.867
for the raptors of "Jurassic Park."

00:07:50.867 --> 00:07:54.420
And so we are here to sort of, in a sense,

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.937
celebrate John Ostrom's work,

00:07:56.937 --> 00:07:58.560
and so what I'd like to do today

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.537
is show you how we get inside
the heads of dinosaurs.

00:08:02.537 --> 00:08:04.262
If we're interested in dinosaurs

00:08:04.262 --> 00:08:06.246
as living, breathing animals,

00:08:06.246 --> 00:08:10.261
we do have to sort of look
beyond the bones themselves

00:08:10.261 --> 00:08:13.833
to see what the bones can tell
us about the soft tissues,

00:08:13.833 --> 00:08:18.237
the muscles, blood vessels,
nerves, brains, skin,

00:08:18.237 --> 00:08:21.168
the clothe that animate the skeleton,

00:08:21.168 --> 00:08:26.168
the features and the biology
that time has stripped away.

00:08:28.919 --> 00:08:31.530
Really, ultimately it comes down to

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:33.611
how do we go from this to this?

00:08:33.611 --> 00:08:35.170
From a pile of bones

00:08:35.170 --> 00:08:38.550
to a fully functioning biological entity?

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:39.900
And really more to the point,

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:41.509
what kind of science is involved

00:08:41.509 --> 00:08:44.945
in reconstructing the
biological attributes

00:08:44.945 --> 00:08:46.856
of extinct animals?

00:08:46.856 --> 00:08:48.720
Well, where do we start?

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:52.620
We start with bones in the
ground, which we remove

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:57.600
and carefully clean and which
we may mount in museums.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:59.688
We still kind of have a pile of bones,

00:08:59.688 --> 00:09:02.710
although maybe arranged in
a more life-like manner.

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:05.582
In many cases, the bones themselves

00:09:05.582 --> 00:09:08.196
hide away their secrets within the rock

00:09:08.196 --> 00:09:10.650
in which they're entombed.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:13.410
And I call all of these dinosaurs here,

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:17.490
analog dinosaurs, 'cause they
live in real physical space

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:21.510
and you can touch 'em and
smell 'em and taste them,

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:23.362
and I love analog dinosaurs,

00:09:23.362 --> 00:09:26.670
but there's a lot that
you can't do with them.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:29.766
And so we've made the jump
into the digital realm

00:09:29.766 --> 00:09:32.796
where you can take a
skull like this T-rex here

00:09:32.796 --> 00:09:37.796
and run it through a CT
scanner at your local hospital,

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:41.880
or in this case, one of NASA's
rocket engine CAT scanners.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:45.072
And that step transfers
that analog dinosaur

00:09:45.072 --> 00:09:47.031
into the digital realm.

00:09:47.031 --> 00:09:48.780
And once it's in your computer,

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:51.900
you can spin it around and
look at from all angles,

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:52.890
which is pretty handy

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:56.070
'cause a lot of these
guys get pretty heavy.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:57.988
But the real advantage in all of this

00:09:57.988 --> 00:10:01.890
is that you can look
inside all of those secrets

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:03.665
that have been kept away from our eyes

00:10:03.665 --> 00:10:06.690
for millions of years are now revealed.

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:10.119
That dusty pile of bones
starts to come alive,

00:10:10.119 --> 00:10:14.147
brimming even, dripping with biology.

00:10:14.147 --> 00:10:18.346
So if soft tissues are so important,

00:10:18.346 --> 00:10:22.740
and dinosaur fossils are
just a collection of bones,

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:24.833
how do we get that soft tissue information

00:10:24.833 --> 00:10:26.940
that is so important?

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:29.070
Well, obviously we need to look at animals

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:32.460
that still have all of
those soft tissues intact

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:35.280
and those are animals living today.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:38.250
So here are what we extant animals.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.824
So here is dinosaurs
typified by T Rex, of course,

00:10:41.824 --> 00:10:46.824
and where it fits into
phylogeny or the tree of life.

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:50.337
Birds are most closely related.

00:10:50.337 --> 00:10:53.054
Birds are in fact a kind of dinosaur.

00:10:53.054 --> 00:10:57.480
And then things like crocodilians
and turtles and lizards

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:00.129
and mammals are more distantly related.

00:11:00.129 --> 00:11:04.059
But really the present
is the key to the past.

00:11:04.059 --> 00:11:06.687
It's kind of a slogan,
but what it really means

00:11:06.687 --> 00:11:09.287
is that if you're interested in dinosaurs,

00:11:09.287 --> 00:11:12.276
you can't just look at dinosaurs,

00:11:12.276 --> 00:11:14.015
you gotta have one foot in the past,

00:11:14.015 --> 00:11:16.380
but you got to have the other foot

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:19.301
firmly planted in the present.

00:11:19.301 --> 00:11:24.150
And so our methods run from the classical

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:25.244
to the cutting edge,

00:11:25.244 --> 00:11:29.880
we use such time-honored
old fashioned techniques

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:34.569
like the section and vascular
injection and sectioning,

00:11:34.569 --> 00:11:39.569
trying to understand the structures

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:42.210
of modern day relatives of dinosaurs,

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:45.180
really trying to give
us ideas and information

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:48.750
on the relationships of the
soft tissues to the bones

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:52.290
that we can then check
for in the dinosaurs.

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:53.991
And one thing I'll quickly point out

00:11:53.991 --> 00:11:56.010
is that all the animals,
all the modern day animals

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:59.070
that we get in are what are
called salvage specimens,

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:01.980
salvage carcasses that died somewhere else

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:03.780
for some other reason or were euthanized

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:07.412
and then were donated
to our lab for research.

00:12:07.412 --> 00:12:11.460
But we also use the latest technology

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:16.403
with CT scanning to get
inside the heads of dinosaurs.

00:12:16.403 --> 00:12:19.050
CT scanning is an x-ray modality

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:21.240
developed for use in hospitals

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:23.820
and we do a lot of scanning at hospitals,

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:28.260
but many universities like
Ohio University and Yale

00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:31.398
have high resolution micro-CT scanners

00:12:31.398 --> 00:12:34.706
that are also very helpful for us.

00:12:34.706 --> 00:12:38.074
So CT is actually a wonderful thing

00:12:38.074 --> 00:12:42.600
if you want to visualize bones
and teeth, the hard tissues.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:44.730
But one of the problems with CT

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:48.300
is that it's not so great
at visualizing soft tissues.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.300
So if we take this lovely
little crocodile skull

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:53.970
or head here and run a slice through it,

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:55.602
you can see that all the soft tissues

00:12:55.602 --> 00:13:00.420
basically just wind up being
a sort of indistinct gray.

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:04.560
We need some way to enhance the contrast

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:08.643
so we can actually see
what's going on in 3D.

00:13:08.643 --> 00:13:11.460
So it turns out that administering iodine

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:14.640
has been a game changer for the study

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:17.010
of a lot of this morphology,

00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:20.040
because iodine differential binds

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:23.370
to different soft tissues
such that we can now see,

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:27.050
it's not indent gray, we can
see just about everything.

00:13:27.050 --> 00:13:30.918
It's really quite remarkable
the detail that we can get.

00:13:30.918 --> 00:13:32.850
Well, we don't see exactly everything

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:33.720
that we'd like to see,

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:37.860
because our lab is also
interested in blood vessels,

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:39.525
the blood vascular system

00:13:39.525 --> 00:13:43.080
and how blood is shunted around the body.

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:45.870
And we'll talk about this a
fair amount as we go in today.

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:49.200
So for example, what we do
is we can take a carcass

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:51.923
that was donated to us of
a pelican, in this case,

00:13:51.923 --> 00:13:56.923
and inject its, we can use
barium, which is a chemical

00:13:57.450 --> 00:14:01.020
that shows up on CT scans
and we can inject it

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:02.760
into the arteries and veins

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:04.710
and then subject it to CT scanning.

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:08.550
So if we look at some slices
here through this pelican,

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:09.570
we can see that we've got

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:12.840
nice bright white arteries and gray veins,

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:15.690
so we can distinguish
between arteries and veins.

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:18.270
You can see that the muscle and the bone

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:21.270
is much less dense and
sort of a darker gray.

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:22.755
If we look closer, we can actually see

00:14:22.755 --> 00:14:25.980
that we've got veins
surrounded by arteries

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:28.560
as well as arteries within veins.

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:29.764
That's really exciting for us,

00:14:29.764 --> 00:14:32.190
because it opens up the possibility

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:35.190
of having some physiological role here,

00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:37.207
particularly with regard to heat exchange

00:14:37.207 --> 00:14:40.470
and shuttling heat and
cool around the body.

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:42.870
Again, this is something
we'll talk about today

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:44.340
when we get to our dinosaurs.

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:47.935
And so we've been using these techniques,

00:14:47.935 --> 00:14:51.600
for example, our vascular techniques.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:55.290
We starting to explore the
physiological significance

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:58.110
of these using thermal imaging.

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:00.750
And so in this case, we
were actually following up

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:03.990
on the pelican example,
we're able to actually show

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:08.990
how blood that's cooled in
the pouch of the pelican

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:11.095
actually can be routed back to the brain

00:15:11.095 --> 00:15:13.578
to help moderate brain temperatures.

00:15:13.578 --> 00:15:16.620
And so these kinds of approaches wind up

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:19.347
being important for us.

00:15:19.347 --> 00:15:21.168
And so this is just a summary slide

00:15:21.168 --> 00:15:26.168
showing the various kinds of
techniques and visualizations

00:15:28.127 --> 00:15:30.120
that we can do.

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:32.730
We still do lots of old school dissection.

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:33.810
We do that all the time.

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:35.580
It's absolutely essential.

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:38.640
But these new techniques
have been so important.

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:40.620
We scientists often like to throw around

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:42.492
the term transformative,

00:15:42.492 --> 00:15:44.654
particularly in our grant proposals,

00:15:44.654 --> 00:15:46.567
(audience laughs)

00:15:46.567 --> 00:15:51.420
but CT scanning has
really been transformative

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:55.980
for the science of anatomy and morphology

00:15:55.980 --> 00:15:57.839
and how it functions.

00:15:57.839 --> 00:16:02.839
So how can these techniques
to help us understand

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:08.397
soft tissues help us
actually flesh out dinosaurs?

00:16:08.397 --> 00:16:10.753
Hollywood can just make stuff up.

00:16:10.753 --> 00:16:15.753
Looking at you "Jurassic
World," but as scientists,

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:19.121
we need testable hypotheses

00:16:19.121 --> 00:16:22.245
grounded in the structure of the fossils

00:16:22.245 --> 00:16:25.830
as well as their modern day relatives.

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:27.381
Well, let's have a look and focus on

00:16:27.381 --> 00:16:32.334
this long neck sauropod
dinosaur, diplodocus.

00:16:32.334 --> 00:16:36.630
So of course we start
with the fossil itself,

00:16:36.630 --> 00:16:41.630
but the fossils are often distorted

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:45.343
or they're missing
parts or they're broken,

00:16:45.343 --> 00:16:48.360
whether by nature or by human hands.

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:53.220
What we really want is
the actual pristine skull

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:56.550
that was in the animal's
head when it was alive

00:16:56.550 --> 00:16:59.550
so that we can build the
soft tissues on that.

00:16:59.550 --> 00:17:01.968
This skull looks pretty nice and it is,

00:17:01.968 --> 00:17:03.810
but it's been living under a mountain

00:17:03.810 --> 00:17:05.940
for the past 150 million years

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:07.920
and is just kind of beat up.

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:11.033
So what we can do then is by CT scanning,

00:17:11.033 --> 00:17:16.033
we can pull that analog skull
into a digital environment

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:17.640
and once it's a computer,

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:20.415
hopefully we can start to fix it

00:17:20.415 --> 00:17:24.299
to reverse the ravaging
effects of of time.

00:17:24.299 --> 00:17:28.080
That is, we can then use computer software

00:17:28.080 --> 00:17:32.670
like Maya or ZBrush to correct distortion

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:37.533
to replace missing bones,
to fix broken elements.

00:17:38.929 --> 00:17:42.168
Starting to bring that thing back into

00:17:42.168 --> 00:17:47.168
what we might call the
skull that the animal had.

00:17:47.371 --> 00:17:49.890
And this winds up being a key thing.

00:17:49.890 --> 00:17:51.835
Now you may quibble about
any of the decisions

00:17:51.835 --> 00:17:55.470
that we made in making this skull,

00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:58.818
but given the vast variation
within the natural population

00:17:58.818 --> 00:18:01.980
of diplodocus 150 million years ago,

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:03.660
I'm pretty sure there was a skull

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:06.436
that looked pretty much just like this.

00:18:06.436 --> 00:18:10.660
In fact, this skull is
really no longer CM11161.

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:15.480
It's basically just
diplodocus at this point.

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:17.730
And so now that we've
got a complete skull,

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:20.752
we can start to hang
some soft tissues on it.

00:18:20.752 --> 00:18:25.752
So what we're gonna do is
sort of try to understand

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:28.530
how we can flesh out this head.

00:18:28.530 --> 00:18:30.810
And now if you think about
it, within our heads,

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:35.040
there's sort of two classes
of soft tissues that we have.

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:37.680
We've got those that are sort
of sitting within cavities

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:41.340
like our brain or the things
within our nasal cavity,

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:45.930
and we have things that span
bones, like the jaw muscle.

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:48.720
Well, let's start with the
first one, things within bones,

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:50.640
because those are a little easier.

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:52.980
So we can take a skull, I like diplodocus,

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:54.150
it's actually a different skull

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:55.872
than the one we just looked at.

00:18:55.872 --> 00:18:58.830
And we can run it through a CT scanner

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:01.911
like we did here and
then digitally slice it,

00:19:01.911 --> 00:19:06.870
revealing a really nice cavity
in the back of the skull

00:19:06.870 --> 00:19:08.790
that's filled with rock.

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:10.830
We can then use software

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:14.151
to digitally isolate or highlight,

00:19:14.151 --> 00:19:17.911
segment is the term we
use in the business here,

00:19:17.911 --> 00:19:21.570
that structure that we
now call a brain endocast.

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:23.670
We then use the information we gained

00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:25.440
and experience gained from looking at

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:28.830
the modern day relatives
of dinosaurs to figure out

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:31.260
what parts of that pertain to the brain

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:34.238
versus nerves versus blood vessels.

00:19:34.238 --> 00:19:39.238
We can do the same thing
with our modeled diplodocus,

00:19:40.140 --> 00:19:42.420
and you can actually see
this is again based on

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:44.767
numerous specimens where we've looked at

00:19:44.767 --> 00:19:47.040
not just that one specimen,

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:49.345
but every specimen we could find.

00:19:49.345 --> 00:19:52.650
We're constantly referring to things

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:57.650
like the specimens cast of
them, CT scan data, photographs,

00:19:57.810 --> 00:20:00.101
the literature that allows
us to restore these things

00:20:00.101 --> 00:20:03.440
such that now that we can
restore the brain endocast

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:06.750
of diplodocus completely and
we can actually extract it,

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:09.840
view it from all angles
and even 3D print it

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:11.580
if we so choose.

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:16.410
And so if we think about some of these

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:17.820
well constrained structures,

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:19.380
they include what we just talked about,

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:22.395
which is this brain
endocast in the back there,

00:20:22.395 --> 00:20:25.325
also some other pretty
well constrained structures

00:20:25.325 --> 00:20:28.170
like a cartilaginous nasal septum,

00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:29.460
the same septum that we have,

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:32.207
like somebody has a deviated
septum, it's the same septum

00:20:32.207 --> 00:20:35.640
as well as this air sinus right in there.

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:39.330
And also portions of things
like blood vessels and nerves

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:43.440
that sort of lie within
grooves on the bone

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:46.083
or actually tunnel
through it in bony canals.

00:20:46.083 --> 00:20:49.020
But we also have less
constrained structures.

00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:51.330
These are the harder
ones to a certain extent,

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:53.724
like some of the blood vessels and nerves

00:20:53.724 --> 00:20:56.280
actually pass through open space.

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:58.696
Those parts are a little
harder for us to reconstruct.

00:20:58.696 --> 00:21:01.126
Also things like the jaw muscles

00:21:01.126 --> 00:21:04.920
that run from the skull
down to the lower jaw.

00:21:04.920 --> 00:21:07.114
Also things like the eyeball

00:21:07.114 --> 00:21:10.650
and the other contents
within the eye socket

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:12.450
as well as the airway.

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:14.367
And there's more things
that we could add in here

00:21:14.367 --> 00:21:17.130
and in fact are, but you can get a sense

00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:19.094
as to how all these soft tissues

00:21:19.094 --> 00:21:22.847
actually share space within the skull.

00:21:22.847 --> 00:21:27.750
Actually all of these ideas here,

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:31.320
all of these soft
tissues that you see here

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:33.633
actually constitute hypotheses

00:21:33.633 --> 00:21:38.633
that we can test via
a variety of criteria.

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:42.240
For example, we use bony signatures,

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:44.310
osteological correlates.

00:21:44.310 --> 00:21:48.060
These are things like the
scars that muscles leave

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:50.280
or the canals or grooves in the bones

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:52.620
left by blood vessels and nerves.

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:55.170
We can also use phylogenetics and here

00:21:55.170 --> 00:21:59.790
we're referring to the
genealogical relationships

00:21:59.790 --> 00:22:02.430
of organisms that tell us something

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:04.740
about the expected anatomy.

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:07.080
For example, we'd expect this air sinus

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:09.351
in the front of the snout to be here

00:22:09.351 --> 00:22:11.785
because it's a shared attribute

00:22:11.785 --> 00:22:14.190
of dinosaurs and their relatives.

00:22:14.190 --> 00:22:16.060
So we would expect that to be there.

00:22:16.060 --> 00:22:18.418
And finally, packaging.

00:22:18.418 --> 00:22:21.793
Packaging constraints wind
up being really important.

00:22:21.793 --> 00:22:23.761
The fact that it's all gotta fit

00:22:23.761 --> 00:22:27.000
means that all of the
different soft tissue systems

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:30.570
in a sense tell us about themselves.

00:22:30.570 --> 00:22:32.220
They tell us about each other,

00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:35.340
sort of a process of
reciprocal illumination.

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:36.780
So using these kinds of things,

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:40.440
we can start to flesh these animals out.

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:42.420
A good example of packaging constraints

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:44.973
are in fact the jaw muscles.

00:22:45.899 --> 00:22:48.930
And this work actually wound up being

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:50.884
really important to us
as we try to understand

00:22:50.884 --> 00:22:54.930
the function of the feeding
apparatus in diplodocus.

00:22:54.930 --> 00:22:56.370
'Cause as we were doing this work,

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:59.100
one thing we discovered is
that we actually couldn't get

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:00.120
everything to fit.

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:03.180
We had a problem, particularly
with the jaw muscles.

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:04.410
And so it turns out we, you know,

00:23:04.410 --> 00:23:06.473
made all these very careful
and minute adjustments

00:23:06.473 --> 00:23:09.000
on positions, making sure they were right.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:12.017
But we wound up really having
to adjust the thicknesses

00:23:12.017 --> 00:23:15.090
of the muscles such
that they wound up being

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:17.070
very thin straps.

00:23:17.070 --> 00:23:18.833
And a key thing to remember
about this kind of work

00:23:18.833 --> 00:23:23.550
is that in the real world,
muscles actually bulge

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:26.280
when they contract and that
actually winds up being

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:29.012
a key factor when you're
trying to do this kind of work,

00:23:29.012 --> 00:23:30.780
because in the real world,

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:33.540
muscles can't be allowed
to intersect or overlap.

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:36.390
In the digital world, you
can let things overlap.

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:38.163
In the real world,
that's not gonna happen.

00:23:38.163 --> 00:23:40.328
Also, what shouldn't
happen in the real world

00:23:40.328 --> 00:23:44.217
if a muscle bulges, it shouldn't
cause the skull to explode.

00:23:44.217 --> 00:23:46.530
(audience laughs)

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:49.164
Important safety tip.

00:23:49.164 --> 00:23:52.740
And so that caused us

00:23:52.740 --> 00:23:56.370
to adjust our thicknesses even further,

00:23:56.370 --> 00:23:58.590
which had some pretty
significant implications

00:23:58.590 --> 00:24:01.980
for the biology of these animals.

00:24:01.980 --> 00:24:05.880
Just a quick aside here as a
shout out to some of the team

00:24:05.880 --> 00:24:10.380
that was involved in helping
us with the early phase

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:12.750
of this visible interactive dinosaur work,

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:14.730
our VID diplodocus.

00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:17.070
This is about 10 years ago,

00:24:17.070 --> 00:24:19.780
and I'll point out that
four of the grad students

00:24:19.780 --> 00:24:22.061
in this photo right here and now

00:24:22.061 --> 00:24:24.853
anatomy professors at medical school,

00:24:24.853 --> 00:24:27.270
and this woman right here in the middle,

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:28.926
Kate Kirby O'Connell,

00:24:28.926 --> 00:24:32.226
is now an animation
supervisor at Disney Studios.

00:24:32.226 --> 00:24:36.093
And so she's done amazing things.

00:24:37.080 --> 00:24:40.140
And so using these kinds of techniques,

00:24:40.140 --> 00:24:42.507
we've actually done more
than just diplodocus.

00:24:42.507 --> 00:24:46.410
And so we've actually
restored, more or less,

00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:49.800
full head anatomy in a whole
range of different dinosaurs

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:52.800
and we've got some others
that aren't in this image.

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:54.840
And that I'll also point
out and acknowledge

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:58.700
the role of my longtime research associate

00:24:58.700 --> 00:25:01.320
and actually research collaborator

00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:04.109
on all this, Ryan Ridgely,
and my good friend

00:25:04.109 --> 00:25:08.550
for over 20 years who's
helped me work through

00:25:08.550 --> 00:25:10.650
all this kind of work.

00:25:10.650 --> 00:25:15.120
And so all of these different, in a sense,

00:25:15.120 --> 00:25:17.190
to flesh out all of these things

00:25:17.190 --> 00:25:20.040
actually winds up being
quite a bit of work.

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:24.810
This very tedious process of
iterative hypothesis testing

00:25:24.810 --> 00:25:27.030
and making all of these references

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:29.400
to the structure of the bones

00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:31.830
and the relationships of the soft tissues,

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:36.120
all those bony signatures, but
the reality is we can do it,

00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:39.000
but what's really exciting
is what we can do with it.

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:42.210
That is how we can use these restorations

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:45.300
to start to explore
things like the function,

00:25:45.300 --> 00:25:48.150
physiology, and behavior of these animals.

00:25:48.150 --> 00:25:51.180
What we call higher order inferences.

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:53.490
And an obvious one is one
that we just talked about,

00:25:53.490 --> 00:25:55.688
which is more realistic modeling

00:25:55.688 --> 00:25:58.650
of jaw and muscle movements.

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:00.969
Like just like we saw here in diplodocus.

00:26:00.969 --> 00:26:03.240
And I pointed out that our work

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:07.020
on this sort of restoration of diplodocus

00:26:07.020 --> 00:26:10.740
required us to have really,
really thin muscles,

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:12.496
which was very curious to us.

00:26:12.496 --> 00:26:16.050
At the time, Eric Snively
was a postdoc in my lab

00:26:16.050 --> 00:26:18.720
and Eric is really
interested in engineering

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:20.220
and engineering approaches.

00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:21.750
And so he wanted to have a look at this.

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:24.120
And so what he did was
he actually measured

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:27.840
the lengths and volumes of
all of these jaw muscles

00:26:27.840 --> 00:26:30.270
so he could calculate muscle forces.

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:33.450
And what he found was that the bite,

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:35.670
he was really interested
in using an approach

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:37.980
called finite element analysis,

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:39.990
which is an engineering approach

00:26:39.990 --> 00:26:42.660
that tells us something
about stresses and strains

00:26:42.660 --> 00:26:45.840
in skulls and actually tells
us something about bite force.

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:49.050
And bite force is a
very important parameter

00:26:49.050 --> 00:26:51.810
for any animal really.

00:26:51.810 --> 00:26:56.810
And so what Eric found was that
the bite force of diplodocus

00:26:57.690 --> 00:27:00.570
was actually just a quarter, one fourth,

00:27:00.570 --> 00:27:03.690
of what we thought it was
before we did all this work.

00:27:03.690 --> 00:27:07.080
And so Eric's work has been
very helpful, very important.

00:27:07.080 --> 00:27:11.684
Here is one of these finite
element analysis illustrations,

00:27:11.684 --> 00:27:15.438
an animation showing areas of high stress

00:27:15.438 --> 00:27:19.620
and the hot colors show
where stresses are very high

00:27:19.620 --> 00:27:21.997
and that's gonna have a big impact

00:27:21.997 --> 00:27:24.510
on what it's going to mean for bite force.

00:27:24.510 --> 00:27:26.509
And so our studies showed that diplodocus

00:27:26.509 --> 00:27:28.477
had a much weaker skull

00:27:28.477 --> 00:27:31.020
than what we had thought in the past.

00:27:31.020 --> 00:27:33.750
And in the past, we thought
it had a pretty weak skull.

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:36.570
And so these new findings impact our view

00:27:36.570 --> 00:27:39.510
on what the behavior in
biology of diplodocus

00:27:39.510 --> 00:27:40.593
must have been.

00:27:41.970 --> 00:27:44.370
Another project, again, this one

00:27:44.370 --> 00:27:47.032
was also led by Eric Snively,

00:27:47.032 --> 00:27:51.750
involved looking at restoring
lots of the soft tissues

00:27:51.750 --> 00:27:56.750
in the head and the neck of
a Jurassic predatory dinosaur

00:27:57.420 --> 00:28:01.920
called Allosaurus, based
on a famous specimen

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:04.110
known as Big Al.

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:08.160
And what we used was borrowing
another engineering approach,

00:28:08.160 --> 00:28:09.660
something from robotics,

00:28:09.660 --> 00:28:12.960
called quantitative multi-body dynamics.

00:28:12.960 --> 00:28:15.739
And so we reconstructed, for example,

00:28:15.739 --> 00:28:19.530
things like the air sinuses and the snout

00:28:19.530 --> 00:28:23.693
and the attachments of the neck muscles

00:28:23.693 --> 00:28:27.720
and basically ran all of these analyses.

00:28:27.720 --> 00:28:30.057
And cutting to the chase, one
of the things we discovered

00:28:30.057 --> 00:28:32.670
is that Allosaurus's head and neck

00:28:32.670 --> 00:28:35.961
actually had something called
a low rotational inertia.

00:28:35.961 --> 00:28:39.934
That's jargon, but what it
means is that Allosaurus

00:28:39.934 --> 00:28:42.060
could move its head around

00:28:42.060 --> 00:28:44.992
with a lot of flexibility and control.

00:28:44.992 --> 00:28:47.940
We also found, it was
an unexpected finding,

00:28:47.940 --> 00:28:51.765
that there was an unusual
attachment of a neck muscle

00:28:51.765 --> 00:28:54.270
that indicates that may have had

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:57.450
a sort of an unusual or different approach

00:28:57.450 --> 00:29:00.197
for removing flesh from a carcass

00:29:00.197 --> 00:29:03.930
where it would actually use
sort of a tugging action

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:06.480
like we saw just there
something that we see

00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:10.302
the falcons do today,
this tug or axial tearing

00:29:10.302 --> 00:29:13.470
rather than something like
a lateral shake of the head,

00:29:13.470 --> 00:29:15.240
which is what alligators do

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:17.100
and is in fact what our studies showed

00:29:17.100 --> 00:29:18.900
that T-rex did as well.

00:29:18.900 --> 00:29:21.549
So we're able to use
these kind of approaches

00:29:21.549 --> 00:29:25.757
to kind of drill down onto
some fairly fine grain aspects

00:29:27.669 --> 00:29:31.233
of feeding behavior in this case.

00:29:32.070 --> 00:29:34.800
All right, well let's
actually transition away

00:29:34.800 --> 00:29:37.920
from feeding biomechanics for a second

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:40.083
and go back to physiology.

00:29:40.083 --> 00:29:42.060
One thing I mentioned before

00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:47.060
that these vascular injections
allow us to, in a sense,

00:29:48.452 --> 00:29:50.930
look at patterns of heat flow,

00:29:50.930 --> 00:29:55.675
because the blood vessels
actually shuttle blood around

00:29:55.675 --> 00:29:58.440
the body to warm and cool certain areas.

00:29:58.440 --> 00:30:00.540
This is something we know
from our own experience.

00:30:00.540 --> 00:30:03.147
On a really hot day,
we will become flushed.

00:30:03.147 --> 00:30:04.920
Our our skin will be turned red

00:30:04.920 --> 00:30:09.171
as we shunt blood to the
skin to radiate heat.

00:30:09.171 --> 00:30:11.250
Likewise, what we saw, just to remind you

00:30:11.250 --> 00:30:13.350
of our pelican here, where we discovered

00:30:13.350 --> 00:30:16.170
that there were venous blood

00:30:16.170 --> 00:30:19.440
that was cooled in the
pouch was routed to the head

00:30:19.440 --> 00:30:21.330
to moderate brain temperatures

00:30:21.330 --> 00:30:24.600
and keep the animals from overheating.

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:29.520
And so these vascular injection approaches

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:32.910
really played into our
dinosaur research in a big way

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:36.030
with the work of my
former doctoral student,

00:30:36.030 --> 00:30:40.830
Ruger Porter and his studies
of dinosaur vasculature

00:30:40.830 --> 00:30:42.675
and brain cooling.

00:30:42.675 --> 00:30:45.930
And here's the problem,
and it's very well known.

00:30:45.930 --> 00:30:47.370
Dinosaurs were big.

00:30:47.370 --> 00:30:50.190
They were ridiculously big in many cases.

00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:51.510
What that means is that they had

00:30:51.510 --> 00:30:54.360
a low surface to volume ratio.

00:30:54.360 --> 00:30:58.320
And how you can envision that
is like a big pot of stove

00:30:58.320 --> 00:31:01.050
or a big pot of chili on your stove.

00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:04.410
Large things heat up and
cool down very slowly

00:31:04.410 --> 00:31:06.330
as opposed to like these little dinosaurs,

00:31:06.330 --> 00:31:08.190
you can see there on the right,

00:31:08.190 --> 00:31:10.380
which are sort of more
like your a cup of coffee

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:12.883
that gets cold as soon as
you turn your back on it

00:31:12.883 --> 00:31:16.800
due to their high
surface to volume ratios.

00:31:16.800 --> 00:31:20.550
So dinosaurs had these high heat loads.

00:31:20.550 --> 00:31:22.350
They would've been a big heat sink.

00:31:22.350 --> 00:31:26.670
And one consequence of that is
that hot blood from the core

00:31:26.670 --> 00:31:30.051
would rush to the head
and potentially damage

00:31:30.051 --> 00:31:34.800
the heat sensitive brain and
sensory tissues located there.

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:38.070
And so as a result,
dinosaurs must have had

00:31:38.070 --> 00:31:40.532
some kind of physiological mechanisms

00:31:40.532 --> 00:31:44.160
to protect and cool
those sensitive organs.

00:31:44.160 --> 00:31:46.200
We know that in modern day animals,

00:31:46.200 --> 00:31:48.870
blood vessels form heat exchangers.

00:31:48.870 --> 00:31:52.260
And so Ruger's project was largely to try

00:31:52.260 --> 00:31:57.023
and identify sites of heat
exchange in these dinosaurs.

00:31:57.023 --> 00:32:02.023
Just as the evaporation
of our sweat on a hot day

00:32:03.427 --> 00:32:07.723
cools us down, animals
use evaporative cooling

00:32:07.723 --> 00:32:10.470
just as we do.

00:32:10.470 --> 00:32:13.560
And so we targeted three main sites

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:17.371
of evaporative cooling in
the head that animals have,

00:32:17.371 --> 00:32:20.580
including the mouth or the oral region,

00:32:20.580 --> 00:32:22.474
the nasal region or nose,

00:32:22.474 --> 00:32:25.260
as well as the eyes and eyelids,

00:32:25.260 --> 00:32:27.813
within the eye socket or the orbit.

00:32:28.830 --> 00:32:31.800
As always, what we do is
we ground our inferences

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:36.800
in the the modern day
relatives that we see here,

00:32:36.900 --> 00:32:41.340
and we did, I don't know,
dozens and dozens of injections

00:32:41.340 --> 00:32:42.982
of modern day animals.

00:32:42.982 --> 00:32:46.220
In all cases, trying to document

00:32:46.220 --> 00:32:49.230
not just the blood vessels themselves,

00:32:49.230 --> 00:32:54.230
but also their bony signatures,
the osteological corals,

00:32:54.900 --> 00:32:57.570
the grooves and canals
that we can then check for

00:32:57.570 --> 00:33:00.765
in the dinosaurs so we
can start to reconstruct

00:33:00.765 --> 00:33:04.110
dinosaur vascular anatomy as well.

00:33:04.110 --> 00:33:06.990
And so basically what we found is

00:33:06.990 --> 00:33:10.104
as a modern day species today,

00:33:10.104 --> 00:33:12.510
blood would've been cooled,

00:33:12.510 --> 00:33:14.201
venous blood would've been cooled

00:33:14.201 --> 00:33:17.390
in these same three
sites of heat exchange.

00:33:17.390 --> 00:33:19.950
In the nose, the mouth,
and in the eye socket.

00:33:19.950 --> 00:33:21.660
And sure enough, what we found

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:24.750
were evidence of rich vascular plexes

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:27.689
in the nose and in the roof of the mouth

00:33:27.689 --> 00:33:30.180
where venous blood would've been cooled

00:33:30.180 --> 00:33:33.667
by evaporative cooling and
then routed back to the brain

00:33:33.667 --> 00:33:37.740
via a variety of different routes.

00:33:37.740 --> 00:33:40.980
And so this information
showed us that Majungasaurus

00:33:40.980 --> 00:33:44.640
and Diplodocus indeed,
probably more dinosaurs,

00:33:44.640 --> 00:33:48.300
maybe all dinosaurs, had
the vascular machinery

00:33:48.300 --> 00:33:50.553
to help regulate their brain temperature.

00:33:50.553 --> 00:33:55.500
In a sense, solving or
addressing a problem

00:33:55.500 --> 00:33:58.320
associated with their large body sizes.

00:33:58.320 --> 00:34:00.825
And so although it looks
like all of these dinosaurs

00:34:00.825 --> 00:34:05.825
probably had all these three
sites of heat exchange,

00:34:07.003 --> 00:34:10.500
some dinosaurs or groups of dinosaurs

00:34:10.500 --> 00:34:13.230
actually emphasized
different blood vessels.

00:34:13.230 --> 00:34:15.548
They had different thermal strategies.

00:34:15.548 --> 00:34:20.548
For example, something like
diplodocus and Majungasaurus

00:34:21.431 --> 00:34:24.600
actually seemed to have a
sort of a balance strategy,

00:34:24.600 --> 00:34:27.556
meaning that all three
sites of heat exchange

00:34:27.556 --> 00:34:29.976
actually seem to be important,

00:34:29.976 --> 00:34:33.868
whereas some actually
emphasized one site or another.

00:34:33.868 --> 00:34:36.480
For example, Camarasaurus,

00:34:36.480 --> 00:34:39.510
the large bodied sauropod dinosaur,

00:34:39.510 --> 00:34:44.006
really emphasized the mouth,
the oral site of heat exchange,

00:34:44.006 --> 00:34:46.800
whereas the armored
dinosaur, Euoplacephalus

00:34:46.800 --> 00:34:49.140
here on the left actually did the reverse

00:34:49.140 --> 00:34:52.892
and was very much using
its nose and nasal region

00:34:52.892 --> 00:34:57.892
as a heat exchanger, an air conditioner.

00:34:57.990 --> 00:35:00.300
And so these kinds of approaches

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:02.768
have been very, very helpful.

00:35:02.768 --> 00:35:05.260
As a brief aside,

00:35:05.260 --> 00:35:10.260
I'll point out that actually in 2020,

00:35:11.128 --> 00:35:15.653
National Geographic did a major
roundup of dinosaur research

00:35:15.653 --> 00:35:17.892
and they actually featured this project

00:35:17.892 --> 00:35:22.170
in a two page infographic right here.

00:35:22.170 --> 00:35:23.640
And here are our guys right here

00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:26.681
is Euoplacephalus and
Camarasaurus and Majungasaurus

00:35:26.681 --> 00:35:29.365
And so it was pretty
cool that they did that.

00:35:29.365 --> 00:35:31.434
In fact, I really liked their title

00:35:31.434 --> 00:35:33.630
and so I stole it for my slides.

00:35:33.630 --> 00:35:35.849
Don't tell 'em.
(audience laughs)

00:35:35.849 --> 00:35:37.473
It's cleverer than I am.

00:35:38.520 --> 00:35:43.520
So kind of a similar kind of project

00:35:44.250 --> 00:35:47.910
was actually trying to
understand patterns of airflow

00:35:47.910 --> 00:35:50.250
through the snouts of dinosaurs.

00:35:50.250 --> 00:35:53.758
Ryan Ridgeley and I,
a number of years ago,

00:35:53.758 --> 00:35:58.350
did a project where we're
pretty well nailed down

00:35:58.350 --> 00:35:59.800
where all the airspace were

00:35:59.800 --> 00:36:03.000
within the snouts of a lot of dinosaurs.

00:36:03.000 --> 00:36:05.340
And in some something like Euoplacephalus,

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:10.340
this ankylosaur, had a
really unusual nasal cavity

00:36:12.210 --> 00:36:14.889
with all these twists and turns in it

00:36:14.889 --> 00:36:18.330
that suggesting that it ran right next to

00:36:18.330 --> 00:36:20.850
all those blood vessels
that we just talked about,

00:36:20.850 --> 00:36:24.720
suggesting a potential
role in in heat exchange

00:36:24.720 --> 00:36:28.500
and potentially even metabolic physiology.

00:36:28.500 --> 00:36:30.780
We also modeled airflow with regard to

00:36:30.780 --> 00:36:33.870
the sense of smell, or olfaction,

00:36:33.870 --> 00:36:36.240
as we did here in T-Rex.

00:36:36.240 --> 00:36:39.570
And we like to think this was in advance,

00:36:39.570 --> 00:36:41.040
but this was 15 years ago

00:36:41.040 --> 00:36:43.883
and we were basically just drawing arrows.

00:36:43.883 --> 00:36:47.220
We're in a 3D virtual environment,

00:36:47.220 --> 00:36:49.683
we should be able to to to model it.

00:36:50.760 --> 00:36:52.650
And so actually Jason Bourke,

00:36:52.650 --> 00:36:55.281
another former PhD student in my lab,

00:36:55.281 --> 00:36:58.006
wanted to tackle this project

00:36:58.006 --> 00:37:00.633
using another engineering approach

00:37:00.633 --> 00:37:04.500
called computational fluid dynamics.

00:37:04.500 --> 00:37:06.630
And I think our lab may
have been among the first

00:37:06.630 --> 00:37:11.630
to actually start using CFD on animals.

00:37:11.790 --> 00:37:13.710
And Jason's been very busy over the years

00:37:13.710 --> 00:37:17.070
doing lots of modeling of airflow

00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:20.010
in lots and lots of
different kinds of animals.

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:21.870
And I'll point out that
actually just about

00:37:21.870 --> 00:37:23.250
all of these are firsts,

00:37:23.250 --> 00:37:27.600
the first time that any bird
has had its airflow modeled

00:37:27.600 --> 00:37:31.500
or reptile or extinct animal of any kind.

00:37:31.500 --> 00:37:33.180
And if you look closely
you can actually see

00:37:33.180 --> 00:37:35.190
the air moving around in there

00:37:35.190 --> 00:37:38.070
and it's actually telling us
something about the speed.

00:37:38.070 --> 00:37:39.690
And one of the things that we can see here

00:37:39.690 --> 00:37:41.088
that in all these cases,

00:37:41.088 --> 00:37:44.760
we get faster airflow in the main airway

00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:47.506
as it goes from the nostril
down into the throat

00:37:47.506 --> 00:37:52.506
and much slower airflow in the
back part of the nasal cavity

00:37:52.585 --> 00:37:55.620
where smelling takes place.

00:37:55.620 --> 00:37:57.150
And it's been thought for some time

00:37:57.150 --> 00:38:00.544
that these slower air speeds
in the olfactory chamber

00:38:00.544 --> 00:38:05.340
in a sense allow the
olfactorant molecules.

00:38:05.340 --> 00:38:07.170
These are little particles of smell.

00:38:07.170 --> 00:38:09.090
You may not think about
smell as being particles,

00:38:09.090 --> 00:38:10.667
but there are little particles of smell

00:38:10.667 --> 00:38:13.151
that find their receptors

00:38:13.151 --> 00:38:18.060
and the slower air speeds are
thought to make that easier.

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:19.650
And Jason's research actually,

00:38:19.650 --> 00:38:23.760
his modeling shows that very, very nicely.

00:38:23.760 --> 00:38:28.760
So we wanted to apply this
approach on an extinct animal,

00:38:28.980 --> 00:38:31.725
in this case, and again,
Jason led this project

00:38:31.725 --> 00:38:35.850
and this project focused
on pachycephalosaurs,

00:38:35.850 --> 00:38:38.090
which are the bony headed dinosaurs.

00:38:38.090 --> 00:38:40.547
In this case, Stegoceras.

00:38:40.547 --> 00:38:43.080
Pachycephalosaurs are
actually a great group

00:38:43.080 --> 00:38:46.860
to work on lots of things
that involve soft tissues

00:38:46.860 --> 00:38:48.180
because they ossified

00:38:48.180 --> 00:38:50.271
so many different kinds of soft tissues

00:38:50.271 --> 00:38:53.460
that other kinds of dinosaurs didn't.

00:38:53.460 --> 00:38:55.830
So if we take the skull
and slice it in half,

00:38:55.830 --> 00:38:58.380
we can of course see
this big thick bony dome

00:38:58.380 --> 00:39:01.263
on top of its head that
gives these animals its name,

00:39:01.263 --> 00:39:05.077
but also what we can
see is in the back part

00:39:05.077 --> 00:39:07.557
of the nasal cavity, right in here,

00:39:07.557 --> 00:39:12.557
are preserved unexpectedly a
series of scrolled portions

00:39:16.050 --> 00:39:18.115
of mineralized cartilage.

00:39:18.115 --> 00:39:21.428
These are something you would
actually call turbinates.

00:39:21.428 --> 00:39:25.080
Turbinates, because the
scrolls sort of look like

00:39:25.080 --> 00:39:26.897
the veins on a turbine.

00:39:26.897 --> 00:39:30.690
And so they're in the region
where smelling takes place,

00:39:30.690 --> 00:39:33.120
so we call these old factory turbinates.

00:39:33.120 --> 00:39:35.640
One thing we also found was a ridge

00:39:35.640 --> 00:39:38.430
that runs right down the main
part of the nose right there

00:39:38.430 --> 00:39:41.490
in the main airway that almost certainly

00:39:41.490 --> 00:39:44.550
had some kind of a
turbinate attached to it.

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:47.250
The key thing here is that
that ridge right there

00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:48.900
is in the main airway.

00:39:48.900 --> 00:39:50.370
And so any turbinate in there

00:39:50.370 --> 00:39:52.770
would be a respiratory turbinate.

00:39:52.770 --> 00:39:55.401
And the significance there
is that respiratory turbinate

00:39:55.401 --> 00:40:00.047
have been sort of really
thought to be important

00:40:00.047 --> 00:40:04.500
in metabolic physiology,
because warm-blooded

00:40:04.500 --> 00:40:06.917
or endothermic mammals and birds

00:40:06.917 --> 00:40:11.430
have those kinds of structures,
respiratory turbinates.

00:40:11.430 --> 00:40:14.860
So turns out that we have
all these structures too

00:40:14.860 --> 00:40:18.168
and so we can sort of see the
turbinate there in a skull.

00:40:18.168 --> 00:40:22.430
In life, those are actually
covered with a mucus membrane,

00:40:22.430 --> 00:40:25.110
in which case we call those conchae.

00:40:25.110 --> 00:40:27.777
And these conchae and turbinate

00:40:27.777 --> 00:40:31.200
are actually involved in
warming and humidifying

00:40:31.200 --> 00:40:34.230
and filtering the inspired air,

00:40:34.230 --> 00:40:36.960
but also this is where a
lot of the heat exchange

00:40:36.960 --> 00:40:37.793
is going too.

00:40:37.793 --> 00:40:41.250
Recall, this is one of those
three sites of heat exchange

00:40:41.250 --> 00:40:43.590
along with the oral cavity and the orbit.

00:40:43.590 --> 00:40:47.880
Now the mucosa, the mucus membranes

00:40:47.880 --> 00:40:49.200
are long gone in the fossils.

00:40:49.200 --> 00:40:52.050
That rots away quickly, but we
might be able to get a handle

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:53.730
on the turbinate.

00:40:53.730 --> 00:40:58.730
And so that was what we set
out to do with Stegosaurus

00:40:59.370 --> 00:41:01.880
and we actually tested
a variety of hypotheses

00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:04.950
versus a series of criteria.

00:41:04.950 --> 00:41:07.080
And I'll just focus on one criterion

00:41:07.080 --> 00:41:10.320
because it's sort of interesting in that

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:13.410
there should be some amount
of olfactory airflow,

00:41:13.410 --> 00:41:15.000
some air going back into

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:17.970
that olfactory chamber in Stegosaurus.

00:41:17.970 --> 00:41:19.263
And the reason for that is that we see

00:41:19.263 --> 00:41:22.730
that it's got really large
olfactory bulbs in the brain.

00:41:22.730 --> 00:41:24.930
In other words, the
neural part of the brain

00:41:24.930 --> 00:41:27.480
that's actually associated
with the sense of smell.

00:41:27.480 --> 00:41:28.500
And again, there's also

00:41:28.500 --> 00:41:31.410
those well-developed olfactory turbines.

00:41:31.410 --> 00:41:34.800
So there should be olfactory airflow.

00:41:34.800 --> 00:41:37.753
The kicker was when we
actually ran the analysis

00:41:37.753 --> 00:41:40.410
in the absence of any kind of turbinate

00:41:40.410 --> 00:41:42.471
in that main airway space there,

00:41:42.471 --> 00:41:45.810
we actually had no olfactory airflow.

00:41:45.810 --> 00:41:48.906
In fact the inspired air
just completely bypassed

00:41:48.906 --> 00:41:52.470
the olfactory region,
suggesting that there must be

00:41:52.470 --> 00:41:54.540
some kind of turbinate in there

00:41:54.540 --> 00:41:57.960
if only to act as a baffle to direct air

00:41:57.960 --> 00:42:00.840
towards that old factory region.

00:42:00.840 --> 00:42:04.680
So we can sort of look at
this, the skull right here,

00:42:04.680 --> 00:42:08.400
again, this is cut in half
and we do have evidence,

00:42:08.400 --> 00:42:10.350
we have that nice ridge right there

00:42:10.350 --> 00:42:13.170
that's very similar to the
kind of turbinate ridges

00:42:13.170 --> 00:42:15.073
we see in mammals, in birds.

00:42:15.073 --> 00:42:19.160
And so that gives us the
opportunity to, in a sense build,

00:42:19.160 --> 00:42:21.620
well, actually you can see
where that ridge right here

00:42:21.620 --> 00:42:23.490
as we sliced through the skull right here,

00:42:23.490 --> 00:42:24.960
you can see that ridge,

00:42:24.960 --> 00:42:27.704
that same ridge right
in there in the fossil,

00:42:27.704 --> 00:42:30.840
and what we did then was to actually model

00:42:30.840 --> 00:42:35.270
in a turbinate attaching to
the length of that ridge.

00:42:37.152 --> 00:42:40.830
We actually modeled multiple
different sort of confirmations

00:42:40.830 --> 00:42:42.501
or shapes of that turbinate

00:42:42.501 --> 00:42:46.208
including being scrolled
or being branched,

00:42:46.208 --> 00:42:48.000
which we borrowed actually literally

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:50.608
from a turkey and an ostrich.

00:42:50.608 --> 00:42:54.120
It turns out that the
branched confirmation,

00:42:54.120 --> 00:42:57.879
the turkey turbinate,
actually performed better,

00:42:57.879 --> 00:43:01.080
but the reality is we don't really know

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:02.974
what that turbinate looked like,

00:43:02.974 --> 00:43:05.040
'cause we just have the ridge.

00:43:05.040 --> 00:43:07.383
But it turns out that with the analysis,

00:43:07.383 --> 00:43:08.807
it doesn't really matter,

00:43:08.807 --> 00:43:11.490
because any of the confirmations we used

00:43:11.490 --> 00:43:14.354
actually restored olfactory airflow.

00:43:14.354 --> 00:43:18.675
And so what we can see
here is that the presence

00:43:18.675 --> 00:43:22.230
of the ridge gives us the
general size and position

00:43:22.230 --> 00:43:23.754
of where that turbinate is,

00:43:23.754 --> 00:43:26.681
and the computational
fluid dynamics analysis

00:43:26.681 --> 00:43:28.740
showed us that there must have been

00:43:28.740 --> 00:43:31.079
some kind of turbinate in that position

00:43:31.079 --> 00:43:33.570
regardless of what its shape is.

00:43:33.570 --> 00:43:36.164
And it's kind of interesting
by modeling different shapes

00:43:36.164 --> 00:43:40.470
that the specific shape doesn't
really matter that much.

00:43:40.470 --> 00:43:42.148
So there are things that we can't know,

00:43:42.148 --> 00:43:44.310
but what's useful for
us to try to figure out

00:43:44.310 --> 00:43:47.160
is how much do we actually need to know?

00:43:47.160 --> 00:43:48.420
And so we know enough

00:43:48.420 --> 00:43:51.240
that there must be something
else going on in there.

00:43:51.240 --> 00:43:53.670
And so in the publication we talked about

00:43:53.670 --> 00:43:55.980
the physiological implications of this,

00:43:55.980 --> 00:43:58.243
what it might mean for, you know,

00:43:58.243 --> 00:44:01.920
warm bloodedness and metabolic physiology.

00:44:01.920 --> 00:44:04.050
But it was also, we're
also really interested

00:44:04.050 --> 00:44:06.150
in highlighting, at least to us,

00:44:06.150 --> 00:44:08.940
this this notion that's kind
of obvious in retrospect,

00:44:08.940 --> 00:44:10.950
but overlooked, which is that turbines

00:44:10.950 --> 00:44:15.030
also perform the function
of acting as baffles

00:44:15.030 --> 00:44:19.170
to move airflow throughout
the nasal region.

00:44:19.170 --> 00:44:21.390
So stepping back and just looking at this,

00:44:21.390 --> 00:44:23.105
this is actually, you know, an animal

00:44:23.105 --> 00:44:24.750
that lived during the Cretaceous

00:44:24.750 --> 00:44:27.960
and we're doing some fairly
refined knowledge of,

00:44:27.960 --> 00:44:29.430
or fairly refined studies

00:44:29.430 --> 00:44:32.400
trying to understand how
these animals worked.

00:44:32.400 --> 00:44:35.703
And we can do that once we're
in this digital environment.

00:44:37.200 --> 00:44:40.770
Let's go back to the armored dinosaurs,

00:44:40.770 --> 00:44:42.900
Ankylosaurs, again.

00:44:42.900 --> 00:44:45.900
If you recall I mentioned
that Euoplocephalus

00:44:45.900 --> 00:44:49.170
had this long, really convoluted airway,

00:44:49.170 --> 00:44:50.815
sort of like the crazy straw

00:44:50.815 --> 00:44:54.190
that kids have if you
remember crazy straws.

00:44:54.190 --> 00:44:59.190
And so we were interested in
actually what this might mean

00:44:59.820 --> 00:45:02.460
in terms of heat exchange,

00:45:02.460 --> 00:45:05.820
in terms of warming and
humidifying inspired air,

00:45:05.820 --> 00:45:10.820
and at the same time,
recapturing moisture and heat

00:45:11.280 --> 00:45:12.692
during exhalation.

00:45:12.692 --> 00:45:15.313
And we had a great team to,
to pull this together again,

00:45:15.313 --> 00:45:18.960
we had Jason Burke doing the
computational fluid dynamics

00:45:18.960 --> 00:45:23.070
and Ruger Porter with his
work on the vasculature.

00:45:23.070 --> 00:45:24.420
And this model right here

00:45:24.420 --> 00:45:27.420
shows one of the results of this work,

00:45:27.420 --> 00:45:30.840
which actually shows a
remarkably efficient system

00:45:30.840 --> 00:45:34.740
for actually in fact warming
the air and humidifying it

00:45:34.740 --> 00:45:37.170
on its way down to the lungs.

00:45:37.170 --> 00:45:39.870
So if we look at this slide here,

00:45:39.870 --> 00:45:41.734
which is a a little complicated,

00:45:41.734 --> 00:45:45.240
this actually represents one of the ways

00:45:45.240 --> 00:45:48.240
that we actually assess efficiency.

00:45:48.240 --> 00:45:50.340
In this case we were
looking at the efficiency

00:45:50.340 --> 00:45:55.340
of reclaiming heat when
the animal exhales.

00:45:55.890 --> 00:45:57.630
And actually if we look at this image

00:45:57.630 --> 00:45:59.340
at the top left right there,

00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:02.340
that's basically what the fossil gave us.

00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:04.290
In a sense that's the bony bounded thing.

00:46:04.290 --> 00:46:06.870
If we just fill it up and look at that,

00:46:06.870 --> 00:46:08.790
that's what the fossil looks like.

00:46:08.790 --> 00:46:09.900
And that's actually not bad.

00:46:09.900 --> 00:46:12.589
That's hovering around 70% efficiency.

00:46:12.589 --> 00:46:15.472
One cool thing about being
in in the digital environment

00:46:15.472 --> 00:46:18.030
is that we can actually generate anatomy

00:46:18.030 --> 00:46:19.530
that nature never did.

00:46:19.530 --> 00:46:22.410
And so this top middle image right there

00:46:22.410 --> 00:46:25.230
is one where Jason basically took out

00:46:25.230 --> 00:46:28.680
all the convolutions and made
the nasal cavity much shorter

00:46:28.680 --> 00:46:31.089
such that the airway went from the nostril

00:46:31.089 --> 00:46:34.650
down into the throat just
like we saw in stegosaurus

00:46:34.650 --> 00:46:36.570
and just like in in us.

00:46:36.570 --> 00:46:39.240
And you can see right there
that the efficiency plummets

00:46:39.240 --> 00:46:41.645
to less than 30% if you do that.

00:46:41.645 --> 00:46:43.950
This one at the bottom
is sort of interesting.

00:46:43.950 --> 00:46:46.440
This is one where we said,
"okay, well let's keep

00:46:46.440 --> 00:46:49.770
all the length and we'll just get rid of

00:46:49.770 --> 00:46:50.730
all the convolutions,

00:46:50.730 --> 00:46:53.430
just make it a long
straight narrow thing."

00:46:53.430 --> 00:46:56.370
And what we can see there
is actually this way better,

00:46:56.370 --> 00:46:59.970
at about 60%, it's way
better than the short airway,

00:46:59.970 --> 00:47:03.120
but not quite what we
see up with the fossil,

00:47:03.120 --> 00:47:06.120
the as preserved in the fossil airway,

00:47:06.120 --> 00:47:08.022
which that tells us that the convolutions

00:47:08.022 --> 00:47:10.067
actually make a difference.

00:47:10.067 --> 00:47:14.790
The convolutions probably
relate to vortices or vortexes

00:47:14.790 --> 00:47:17.030
of air, and as they go
around those curves,

00:47:17.030 --> 00:47:20.550
that actually apparently
makes a difference.

00:47:20.550 --> 00:47:22.113
On the top right there though,

00:47:22.113 --> 00:47:25.380
that's one where we
actually went a step further

00:47:25.380 --> 00:47:27.570
and actually restored the soft tissues.

00:47:27.570 --> 00:47:31.668
We actually put the
mucus membranes back in

00:47:31.668 --> 00:47:34.570
and at that point the efficiency sores up

00:47:34.570 --> 00:47:38.765
to around 80 plus percent right there.

00:47:38.765 --> 00:47:42.660
So these kinds of approaches
allow us to, in a sense,

00:47:42.660 --> 00:47:47.318
do simulations and experiments
within a computer environment

00:47:47.318 --> 00:47:51.689
that give us a glimmer
into how these animals

00:47:51.689 --> 00:47:53.641
may have actually worked.

00:47:53.641 --> 00:47:56.040
And stepping back a little bit,

00:47:56.040 --> 00:47:57.720
one of the things that's
sort of interesting

00:47:57.720 --> 00:48:01.534
is that ankylosaurs like Europlocephalus

00:48:01.534 --> 00:48:05.190
don't actually have turbinate
like we saw in stegosaurus

00:48:05.190 --> 00:48:08.070
or people in a in a whole
bunch of of other animals.

00:48:08.070 --> 00:48:09.420
But what they may have actually

00:48:09.420 --> 00:48:11.335
by having this long convoluted airway

00:48:11.335 --> 00:48:15.851
is in a sense an alternate
evolutionary solution

00:48:15.851 --> 00:48:19.170
to the same physiological problem.

00:48:19.170 --> 00:48:22.190
A lot of things ultimately
just matter about surface area,

00:48:22.190 --> 00:48:25.738
an expanding surface area
no matter how you do it.

00:48:25.738 --> 00:48:29.798
Also, we can see how well
Ruger Porter's work jived

00:48:29.798 --> 00:48:33.300
with Jason's work and that we can see

00:48:33.300 --> 00:48:35.554
that these blood vessels,

00:48:35.554 --> 00:48:38.010
the veins would've actually been cooled

00:48:38.010 --> 00:48:41.970
by evaporative cooling as
they're going past all of those,

00:48:41.970 --> 00:48:45.960
the mucosa within those
that convoluted airway,

00:48:45.960 --> 00:48:47.969
and then that cooled blood routes back

00:48:47.969 --> 00:48:51.573
towards the brain to help
moderate brain temperatures.

00:48:52.860 --> 00:48:56.983
And I think John Ostrom
would've loved all this.

00:48:56.983 --> 00:49:01.650
I think he would've, John
was actually a geologist,

00:49:01.650 --> 00:49:04.409
but he was also a hardcore
anatomist as well.

00:49:04.409 --> 00:49:09.409
And so going back to his
doctoral dissertation in 1961

00:49:10.067 --> 00:49:13.560
that was looking at these crazy crests

00:49:13.560 --> 00:49:17.010
on the heads of duckbill
dinosaurs like Corythosaurus,

00:49:17.010 --> 00:49:19.800
he actually did a lot of
work with modern day animals.

00:49:19.800 --> 00:49:22.306
So he was looking at things
like jaw muscles and brains

00:49:22.306 --> 00:49:25.412
and airways in animals
that are around today

00:49:25.412 --> 00:49:28.290
so that he could interpret his fossils.

00:49:28.290 --> 00:49:30.480
That's exactly what we do today,

00:49:30.480 --> 00:49:32.460
we've just got different tools,

00:49:32.460 --> 00:49:35.070
we've got more advanced tools to do that.

00:49:35.070 --> 00:49:37.650
And John's work has been
absolutely foundational

00:49:37.650 --> 00:49:40.740
for work that we've
done sort of, you know,

00:49:40.740 --> 00:49:43.460
21st century kind of stuff looking at

00:49:43.460 --> 00:49:47.510
the structure and
development of these airways

00:49:47.510 --> 00:49:50.330
and as well as the brain in Corythosaurus

00:49:50.330 --> 00:49:52.770
as well as some of its other animals.

00:49:52.770 --> 00:49:54.660
And this is a good sort of lead in

00:49:54.660 --> 00:49:59.660
to another benefit of these
very visual 3D restorations

00:50:03.553 --> 00:50:06.989
is that it's really good way
to reach out to the public

00:50:06.989 --> 00:50:09.990
with our science because
they're so visible

00:50:09.990 --> 00:50:11.910
and so easy to understand.

00:50:11.910 --> 00:50:15.330
Again, in the the 2020
National Geographic,

00:50:15.330 --> 00:50:18.270
they did another infographic on our work,

00:50:18.270 --> 00:50:20.640
in this case of a cousin of Corythosaurus

00:50:20.640 --> 00:50:24.390
called Hypachrysaurus
looking at the airway,

00:50:24.390 --> 00:50:26.802
but also the brain and sensory systems

00:50:26.802 --> 00:50:30.090
and our also our work on Parasaurolophus,

00:50:30.090 --> 00:50:33.161
another cousin of Corythosaurus

00:50:33.161 --> 00:50:36.983
will actually be appearing
soon in a TV documentary

00:50:36.983 --> 00:50:39.840
devoted to some other things.

00:50:39.840 --> 00:50:43.260
And that picture at the lower
right there shows me holding

00:50:43.260 --> 00:50:47.700
the 3D prints of the skull
and airway of Parasaurolophus

00:50:47.700 --> 00:50:49.050
which probably would've just been

00:50:49.050 --> 00:50:51.180
an unbelievable thrill for John Ostrom

00:50:51.180 --> 00:50:53.763
to have been able to
do something like that.

00:50:54.630 --> 00:50:59.630
So in summary, the work
of people like John Ostrom

00:51:00.785 --> 00:51:04.546
remains as vital today
as it as it ever has,

00:51:04.546 --> 00:51:07.977
and we're just in a sense building on it.

00:51:07.977 --> 00:51:12.030
The modern technology has not
replaced it or cast it aside.

00:51:12.030 --> 00:51:16.027
Rather, we're using it
as a foundation for us

00:51:16.027 --> 00:51:18.836
to actually use these technologies

00:51:18.836 --> 00:51:23.836
to actually start to understand
the biology of dinosaurs

00:51:24.570 --> 00:51:28.340
by basically putting them
back together in a computer.

00:51:28.340 --> 00:51:30.450
And at the same time that really gives us

00:51:30.450 --> 00:51:33.300
a new opportunity to
reach out to the public

00:51:33.300 --> 00:51:34.920
and talk to them about science

00:51:34.920 --> 00:51:37.260
just like we're doing right now.

00:51:37.260 --> 00:51:39.249
And thanks for your attention today.

00:51:39.249 --> 00:51:42.249
(audience applauds)

00:51:43.620 --> 00:51:44.610
I'll just quickly point out,

00:51:44.610 --> 00:51:46.590
I took this photo this morning

00:51:46.590 --> 00:51:48.227
when I was in working in the collections

00:51:48.227 --> 00:51:50.673
over in the Peabody Museum.

00:51:51.870 --> 00:51:53.394
- [Moderator] Alright,
thanks so much Larry.

00:51:53.394 --> 00:51:57.173
So we will have a question answer session

00:51:57.173 --> 00:52:01.860
for about maybe say 10 minutes now.

00:52:01.860 --> 00:52:03.780
And what's gonna happen
is I'll call on people

00:52:03.780 --> 00:52:06.090
and if you wait until
someone runs up to you

00:52:06.090 --> 00:52:08.430
with a microphone so everyone
can hear the question,

00:52:08.430 --> 00:52:09.390
that would be great.

00:52:09.390 --> 00:52:10.950
Okay, let's go.

00:52:10.950 --> 00:52:12.259
Yes, over here.

00:52:12.259 --> 00:52:13.323
Please.

00:52:17.580 --> 00:52:18.600
- Thank you very much.

00:52:18.600 --> 00:52:20.910
Just wondering, and
it's not directly to do

00:52:20.910 --> 00:52:21.750
with what you're doing,

00:52:21.750 --> 00:52:25.560
but you must have thought about
it, what about intelligence?

00:52:25.560 --> 00:52:30.435
Can you say with any
definitiveness that certain species

00:52:30.435 --> 00:52:33.930
were more intelligent than
others because of this and that?

00:52:33.930 --> 00:52:34.890
- Sure, yeah, yeah.

00:52:34.890 --> 00:52:38.006
The question was what can
we say about intelligence

00:52:38.006 --> 00:52:39.539
in these animals?

00:52:39.539 --> 00:52:44.539
And actually I've been
studying brains in dinosaurs

00:52:44.700 --> 00:52:45.533
for a long time.

00:52:45.533 --> 00:52:47.575
We've got a major grant
that's looking at cognition

00:52:47.575 --> 00:52:51.138
in deep time and intelligence

00:52:51.138 --> 00:52:53.571
is a sort of a difficult thing to do

00:52:53.571 --> 00:52:57.205
and a lot of cognitive biologists
prefer the term cognition

00:52:57.205 --> 00:53:00.013
as to how capable are animals

00:53:00.013 --> 00:53:03.540
of solving problems in
learning and things like that.

00:53:03.540 --> 00:53:06.460
We colloquially we would call that smart

00:53:06.460 --> 00:53:08.640
and the reality is there are differences

00:53:08.640 --> 00:53:11.954
among dinosaurs in that capacity.

00:53:11.954 --> 00:53:14.760
We always tend to think that animals

00:53:14.760 --> 00:53:16.428
just need to be smart enough,

00:53:16.428 --> 00:53:20.610
and a key factor in that is that brains

00:53:20.610 --> 00:53:22.875
are very expensive organs.

00:53:22.875 --> 00:53:26.550
Brains actually cost a lot
of energy that we take in.

00:53:26.550 --> 00:53:28.920
And so animals very often need to,

00:53:28.920 --> 00:53:33.840
in a sense, evolutionarily
fine tune their brain size

00:53:33.840 --> 00:53:36.076
so that they're not in
a sense wasting energy

00:53:36.076 --> 00:53:40.080
on cognitive abilities
or neural processing

00:53:40.080 --> 00:53:41.370
that they don't need.

00:53:41.370 --> 00:53:44.850
And so we see animals like T-Rex

00:53:44.850 --> 00:53:46.680
and other predatory dinosaurs tend to have

00:53:46.680 --> 00:53:48.510
relatively large brains

00:53:48.510 --> 00:53:50.400
'cause they're doing a
lot of neural processing

00:53:50.400 --> 00:53:51.750
to be predators.

00:53:51.750 --> 00:53:54.998
Whereas something like
diplodocus, bless his heart,

00:53:54.998 --> 00:53:59.998
I was going there, has
really quite a small brain,

00:54:00.494 --> 00:54:04.110
but the dude was gigantic
and it was just eaten plants

00:54:04.110 --> 00:54:07.245
that aren't moving and so
its cognitive requirements

00:54:07.245 --> 00:54:10.110
were significantly less.

00:54:10.110 --> 00:54:13.800
And so the the notion of
of dinosaur intelligence

00:54:13.800 --> 00:54:16.230
is something that our broader team,

00:54:16.230 --> 00:54:19.650
it's actually a multinational
team, is actually working on.

00:54:19.650 --> 00:54:21.200
So thank you for that question.

00:54:22.170 --> 00:54:23.463
- [Moderator] Up here.

00:54:23.463 --> 00:54:24.296
Top back.

00:54:25.863 --> 00:54:26.793
Way back.

00:54:27.780 --> 00:54:29.423
Saw your hand up this whole time.

00:54:37.140 --> 00:54:40.710
- [Audience Member] Are you
holding a Dinonicus over there?

00:54:40.710 --> 00:54:41.760
- Yeah, yeah.

00:54:41.760 --> 00:54:43.500
That's a Dinonicus,

00:54:43.500 --> 00:54:45.840
same Dinonicus as John Ostrom right there.

00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:46.673
- [Audience Member] Whoa.

00:54:46.673 --> 00:54:48.600
- Yep, and that's a
Dinonicus foot right there

00:54:48.600 --> 00:54:50.250
with the killing claw.

00:54:50.250 --> 00:54:51.083
- [Audience Member] What's that guy?

00:54:51.083 --> 00:54:53.490
I thought that was like Velociraptor.

00:54:53.490 --> 00:54:56.520
- Well, it's very similar to Velociraptor.

00:54:56.520 --> 00:54:59.730
Velociraptor in the
movies is like this big,

00:54:59.730 --> 00:55:01.516
in real life it was about this big.

00:55:01.516 --> 00:55:02.430
- Yeah.

00:55:02.430 --> 00:55:03.300
Before about Hollywood.

00:55:03.300 --> 00:55:05.635
- [Audience Member] I
watched the franchise a lot.

00:55:05.635 --> 00:55:07.500
(audience laughs)

00:55:07.500 --> 00:55:10.074
- You and me both, dude.

00:55:10.074 --> 00:55:12.420
- [Moderator] You can
come see that Dinonicus

00:55:12.420 --> 00:55:14.703
when the museum reopens, it's here.

00:55:15.796 --> 00:55:18.090
Okay, let's see.

00:55:18.090 --> 00:55:22.773
How about the back of the front row here?

00:55:31.149 --> 00:55:33.180
- [Audience Member] Do
you use to determine

00:55:33.180 --> 00:55:37.764
how strong a jaw muscle
to help out determine

00:55:37.764 --> 00:55:39.985
how strong a jaw muscle would be,

00:55:39.985 --> 00:55:43.859
do you use whether they
would be a carnivore

00:55:43.859 --> 00:55:46.617
or a well, word, yeah, herbivore.

00:55:49.472 --> 00:55:50.741
That.

00:55:50.741 --> 00:55:53.480
That word that I can't pronounce yet.

00:55:53.480 --> 00:55:54.780
Okay.

00:55:54.780 --> 00:55:55.964
Well, I normally can.

00:55:55.964 --> 00:56:00.964
Because the carnivores
might need more jaw muscle

00:56:01.980 --> 00:56:06.480
to actually tear the flesh off

00:56:06.480 --> 00:56:11.480
whereas the herbivores
wouldn't need to do that.

00:56:12.145 --> 00:56:13.067
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:56:13.067 --> 00:56:14.730
So the question was,

00:56:14.730 --> 00:56:16.410
well, what kind of muscle force

00:56:16.410 --> 00:56:19.410
does it take to be a a
predator or an herbivore?

00:56:19.410 --> 00:56:22.360
And the answer as is so true
in much of life is it depends.

00:56:25.350 --> 00:56:28.167
And it depends on what
kind of a carnivore you are

00:56:28.167 --> 00:56:29.580
and what kind of an herbivore you are.

00:56:29.580 --> 00:56:33.304
So for example, if you're T-rex,

00:56:33.304 --> 00:56:36.780
an adult T-rex and you are biting bones,

00:56:36.780 --> 00:56:38.536
you need big powerful muscles

00:56:38.536 --> 00:56:41.850
to actually crack through those bones.

00:56:41.850 --> 00:56:43.960
If you are Dinonicus,

00:56:43.960 --> 00:56:48.960
your skull is probably adapted
more for removing flesh,

00:56:49.620 --> 00:56:52.290
using other ways to kill the animal,

00:56:52.290 --> 00:56:55.620
and Dinonicus is actually
a fairly delicate animal

00:56:55.620 --> 00:56:56.760
in many ways.

00:56:56.760 --> 00:57:01.050
And so it was probably not cracking bones,

00:57:01.050 --> 00:57:02.700
something like Allosaurus,

00:57:02.700 --> 00:57:04.037
we talked about Allosaurus

00:57:04.037 --> 00:57:06.330
and how it was actually pulling back.

00:57:06.330 --> 00:57:08.798
And in that case when it was
removing flesh from the bone,

00:57:08.798 --> 00:57:11.338
it was actually just
holding on with its teeth

00:57:11.338 --> 00:57:13.310
and then muscles that
were actually working

00:57:13.310 --> 00:57:15.660
were the neck muscles to pull that back.

00:57:15.660 --> 00:57:18.180
Same thing with some of the plant eaters,

00:57:18.180 --> 00:57:20.820
something like Diplodocus right here,

00:57:20.820 --> 00:57:23.370
basically its teeth are kind of a joke.

00:57:23.370 --> 00:57:25.200
They're just these little,

00:57:25.200 --> 00:57:26.370
they look like little crayons

00:57:26.370 --> 00:57:28.170
that are jammed in the front of its face

00:57:28.170 --> 00:57:29.790
that were probably as sort of as rakes

00:57:29.790 --> 00:57:31.980
or something to strip branches off.

00:57:31.980 --> 00:57:35.169
They were not chewing and
so they had relatively

00:57:35.169 --> 00:57:38.820
super weak jaws and bite force.

00:57:38.820 --> 00:57:41.016
Whereas there's other
dinosaurs, other plant eaters,

00:57:41.016 --> 00:57:43.501
that have really powerful bites,

00:57:43.501 --> 00:57:45.600
and particularly if there's animals

00:57:45.600 --> 00:57:48.120
that are doing things
like biting repeatedly,

00:57:48.120 --> 00:57:50.730
like chewing or cracking seeds,

00:57:50.730 --> 00:57:54.000
that requires big, big jaw muscles.

00:57:54.000 --> 00:57:57.720
A cardinal at your feeder
has really big jaw muscles

00:57:57.720 --> 00:58:00.180
'cause it's cracking
seeds whereas a sparrow

00:58:00.180 --> 00:58:02.010
at your feeder doesn't,
'cause it's just picking up

00:58:02.010 --> 00:58:04.350
little, little seeds
and chucking them down.

00:58:04.350 --> 00:58:07.620
So it depends, but it's a great question.

00:58:07.620 --> 00:58:08.453
Thank you.

00:58:09.540 --> 00:58:12.093
- [Moderator] Oh, bottom here in the blue.

00:58:16.890 --> 00:58:18.294
- [Audience Member] How do you figure out

00:58:18.294 --> 00:58:20.256
how to do all of that?

00:58:20.256 --> 00:58:24.232
- Yeah.
(audience laughs)

00:58:24.232 --> 00:58:26.470
- That may be the best question we get.

00:58:26.470 --> 00:58:29.730
How do we figure out how to do all that?

00:58:29.730 --> 00:58:31.250
I spent a lot of time explaining that

00:58:31.250 --> 00:58:33.090
to that thing that says NSF up there.

00:58:33.090 --> 00:58:35.100
(audience laughs)

00:58:35.100 --> 00:58:36.210
That's the funding agency.

00:58:36.210 --> 00:58:37.140
But that's a great question.

00:58:37.140 --> 00:58:38.250
I don't mean to make light of it,

00:58:38.250 --> 00:58:40.770
because actually it's kind of hard

00:58:40.770 --> 00:58:42.240
to do all this these things

00:58:42.240 --> 00:58:44.520
and there's lots and lots of steps

00:58:44.520 --> 00:58:47.160
and you saw that I had a lot
of people that I work with,

00:58:47.160 --> 00:58:49.890
I work a lot with with graduate students

00:58:49.890 --> 00:58:51.600
and undergraduate students, I've even had

00:58:51.600 --> 00:58:54.450
high school students working
in my lab and post-docs,

00:58:54.450 --> 00:58:57.780
and we work as a team and we
often do different parts of it.

00:58:57.780 --> 00:59:01.110
Like you saw, I had one person
that was working really hard

00:59:01.110 --> 00:59:03.270
on blood vessels and trying
to figure out where they went.

00:59:03.270 --> 00:59:04.620
Another person that was
trying to figure out

00:59:04.620 --> 00:59:06.269
how airflow works.

00:59:06.269 --> 00:59:09.580
And so all of these team
members get together

00:59:09.580 --> 00:59:12.990
and they work as a team.

00:59:12.990 --> 00:59:15.300
But it is hard and it
takes us a while sometimes

00:59:15.300 --> 00:59:17.190
to actually come up with these things.

00:59:17.190 --> 00:59:18.023
Thank you.

00:59:18.023 --> 00:59:18.856
That's a great question.

00:59:20.490 --> 00:59:22.437
- [Moderator] Lisa, I
have it right next to you.

00:59:27.507 --> 00:59:31.260
- You talked about Euoplacephalus

00:59:31.260 --> 00:59:34.170
and it's very complicated nasal passages.

00:59:34.170 --> 00:59:36.603
Now, in more recent days I've seen

00:59:36.603 --> 00:59:39.600
sort of interesting
speculative reconstructions

00:59:39.600 --> 00:59:42.211
of Euoplacephalus and
similar inchyalosaurs

00:59:42.211 --> 00:59:46.410
with like nasal air sacs
attached to the nostrils

00:59:46.410 --> 00:59:50.810
that will inflate and possibly
make a sound to communicate.

00:59:50.810 --> 00:59:52.260
Your thoughts on that.

00:59:52.260 --> 00:59:53.550
Is that plausible or?

00:59:53.550 --> 00:59:54.383
- Sure, yeah.

00:59:54.383 --> 00:59:57.930
So the question was some
people have actually suggested

00:59:57.930 --> 01:00:00.570
that some dinosaurs could
have had inflatable sacks

01:00:00.570 --> 01:00:02.014
that are coming out of their nose,

01:00:02.014 --> 01:00:06.603
which sounds pretty crazy,
but animals do crazy things.

01:00:06.603 --> 01:00:10.551
And so I mean what
Euoplacephalus does without that

01:00:10.551 --> 01:00:13.200
is pretty crazy, so if Euoplacephalus

01:00:13.200 --> 01:00:14.472
had inflatable nose sacks,

01:00:14.472 --> 01:00:18.660
that would be maybe right
in line for that animal.

01:00:18.660 --> 01:00:19.920
The reality is some of these things

01:00:19.920 --> 01:00:21.750
are pretty hard for us to get at.

01:00:21.750 --> 01:00:24.150
The person that actually
presented the notion of that

01:00:24.150 --> 01:00:27.330
for I think like triceratops
and its relatives,

01:00:27.330 --> 01:00:29.880
a ceratopsian, I'm not sure he was,

01:00:29.880 --> 01:00:31.920
I think he was being a little
whimsical when he did that.

01:00:31.920 --> 01:00:36.920
But the fact of the matter
is inflatable display organs

01:00:37.034 --> 01:00:41.794
are used all the time
in the animal kingdom.

01:00:41.794 --> 01:00:44.370
I don't know if you've
heard the frogs out yet,

01:00:44.370 --> 01:00:46.770
but you get the peepers and
they'll be actually doing it

01:00:46.770 --> 01:00:49.560
in many species that are
actually brightly colored.

01:00:49.560 --> 01:00:52.110
There are things like elephant
seals and hooded seals

01:00:52.110 --> 01:00:54.960
that actually have inflatable nose bags

01:00:54.960 --> 01:00:56.463
that come off of their,

01:00:57.480 --> 01:00:59.280
apparently the females just love it.

01:01:01.130 --> 01:01:02.535
There's no accounting for taste.

01:01:02.535 --> 01:01:06.600
And so could some of
these dinosaurs have it?

01:01:06.600 --> 01:01:08.329
The answer is maybe,

01:01:08.329 --> 01:01:10.110
and those are the kind of questions

01:01:10.110 --> 01:01:11.837
that actually we are trying to get at.

01:01:11.837 --> 01:01:13.813
One of the things we've discovered

01:01:13.813 --> 01:01:17.060
over the course of time is that a lot of,

01:01:17.060 --> 01:01:20.939
in sort of in some respects
my basic job has become

01:01:20.939 --> 01:01:24.768
is where is the limit of what we can know?

01:01:24.768 --> 01:01:27.807
It turns out a lot of the
really interesting things

01:01:27.807 --> 01:01:29.674
are like right out there.

01:01:29.674 --> 01:01:31.440
They're just beyond the
limits of what we can know.

01:01:31.440 --> 01:01:34.500
What's interesting is that
technology like CT scanning

01:01:34.500 --> 01:01:36.570
and all these other techniques going boom,

01:01:36.570 --> 01:01:38.430
all of a sudden we've
got some new techniques

01:01:38.430 --> 01:01:41.258
that we can start to know
more than what we thought.

01:01:41.258 --> 01:01:44.311
And also just making
the right comparisons.

01:01:44.311 --> 01:01:46.260
Actually starting to look at,

01:01:46.260 --> 01:01:48.700
we've actually dissected
hooded seals and elephant seals

01:01:48.700 --> 01:01:52.447
'cause we have actually are
interested in that question.

01:01:52.447 --> 01:01:57.202
I have come to learn to
never say never about life,

01:01:57.202 --> 01:02:00.510
because life just evolves

01:02:00.510 --> 01:02:04.273
all of these different kinds of organisms.

01:02:04.273 --> 01:02:05.220
Thank you.

01:02:05.220 --> 01:02:06.240
That's a good one.

01:02:06.240 --> 01:02:07.966
- [Moderator] Okay.

01:02:07.966 --> 01:02:10.023
How about front and center here?

01:02:16.505 --> 01:02:19.672
Or at least having you run all around.

01:02:25.260 --> 01:02:26.093
- [Audience Member] Thanks.

01:02:26.093 --> 01:02:29.130
So I guess something I'm wondering about,

01:02:29.130 --> 01:02:31.650
we know that dinosaurs I think at least

01:02:31.650 --> 01:02:33.540
became warm-blooded over time.

01:02:33.540 --> 01:02:36.084
I'd imagine from a heat
exchange standpoint

01:02:36.084 --> 01:02:38.259
those would be, you know,

01:02:38.259 --> 01:02:40.440
kind of different heat exchange problems

01:02:40.440 --> 01:02:42.592
when you're dealing with
a cold-blooded creature

01:02:42.592 --> 01:02:44.300
versus a warm-blooded one.

01:02:44.300 --> 01:02:48.690
Do you see any like evidence of changes

01:02:48.690 --> 01:02:52.410
in sort of the heat exchange
efficiency over time?

01:02:52.410 --> 01:02:56.490
- Well, so the question
was with the evolution

01:02:56.490 --> 01:02:59.198
of warm bloodedness or
endothermic, you know,

01:02:59.198 --> 01:03:01.110
would that occur over time

01:03:01.110 --> 01:03:02.640
and what would we see changes in that?

01:03:02.640 --> 01:03:03.480
It's a great question.

01:03:03.480 --> 01:03:05.570
It's a difficult one, but one
that we've thought a lot about

01:03:05.570 --> 01:03:09.530
and often it's not so much over time,

01:03:09.530 --> 01:03:12.180
often it's within different groups.

01:03:12.180 --> 01:03:14.891
Different groups will often
have different strategies.

01:03:14.891 --> 01:03:19.020
And so the reality is it's really hard

01:03:19.020 --> 01:03:21.690
to be warm-blooded like we are.

01:03:21.690 --> 01:03:24.930
I mean it's actually hard
to maintain body temperature

01:03:24.930 --> 01:03:27.390
at a peak rate and there's lots of costs

01:03:27.390 --> 01:03:30.570
that go around with that,
like breathing fast.

01:03:30.570 --> 01:03:34.320
We breathe faster than
cold blooded animals.

01:03:34.320 --> 01:03:35.430
And that's actually a lot of these things

01:03:35.430 --> 01:03:38.250
that we're looking at in terms
of reclaiming heat and water.

01:03:38.250 --> 01:03:39.703
It's 'cause we breathe really fast

01:03:39.703 --> 01:03:41.741
and you can actually dehydrate yourself

01:03:41.741 --> 01:03:43.893
when you breathe too fast.

01:03:45.660 --> 01:03:48.780
And so we are actually indeed trying

01:03:48.780 --> 01:03:50.490
to look at those structures.

01:03:50.490 --> 01:03:53.026
What's interesting is that a lot of these,

01:03:53.026 --> 01:03:55.837
we think about hot-blooded dinosaurs,

01:03:55.837 --> 01:03:57.450
a lot of the things we're looking at

01:03:57.450 --> 01:04:01.320
are things that are because
dinosaurs are too hot-blooded.

01:04:01.320 --> 01:04:02.370
In other words, endothermia

01:04:02.370 --> 01:04:04.740
makes some of these things worse.

01:04:04.740 --> 01:04:07.980
If you're a big dinosaur like Diplodocus

01:04:07.980 --> 01:04:10.890
or any of these guys where
you're just a giant heat sink

01:04:10.890 --> 01:04:12.431
with the sun coming down on you,

01:04:12.431 --> 01:04:15.746
heating you up, you're gonna be hot

01:04:15.746 --> 01:04:18.619
whether you're generating heat internally

01:04:18.619 --> 01:04:22.188
like a warm blooded, you
know, an endoderm does,

01:04:22.188 --> 01:04:25.135
you're gonna be hot anyways.

01:04:25.135 --> 01:04:29.340
And so these kinds of physio,

01:04:29.340 --> 01:04:31.260
we call them physiological devices,

01:04:31.260 --> 01:04:34.050
things that you can use to, in a sense,

01:04:34.050 --> 01:04:35.910
deal with these heat problems

01:04:35.910 --> 01:04:39.003
are gonna be true for so
many of these dinosaurs.

01:04:39.003 --> 01:04:41.760
What's interesting is we find evidence

01:04:41.760 --> 01:04:43.858
of a pretty legit nasal,

01:04:43.858 --> 01:04:48.858
a respiratory turbinate
in this little Stegosaurus

01:04:48.960 --> 01:04:49.980
which was right down here,

01:04:49.980 --> 01:04:52.500
which doesn't have the
big overheating problem.

01:04:52.500 --> 01:04:55.920
If anything, that guy would
get chilly from time to time.

01:04:55.920 --> 01:04:56.790
It was cold.

01:04:56.790 --> 01:04:58.718
And so there's a decent
chance of something like that

01:04:58.718 --> 01:05:03.718
it were endothermic, it
would've had the machinery

01:05:05.340 --> 01:05:06.940
to actually help deal with that.

01:05:08.550 --> 01:05:09.383
- [Moderator] Okay.

01:05:09.383 --> 01:05:12.630
All right, we're getting
close to past 10 after now,

01:05:12.630 --> 01:05:14.040
and with great apologies.

01:05:14.040 --> 01:05:16.263
I think we're gonna have to cut it off.

01:05:18.390 --> 01:05:19.668
Thank you so much.

01:05:19.668 --> 01:05:22.418
(audience claps)

